{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And Moses said, etc.</b> Perhaps Moses derived this commandment on his own, for he saw that HaShem was giving them their daily portion day by day, and this indicated that no day prepares for the next, and he inferred one matter from within another and forbade it to them. Therefore the verse says ״וְלֹא שָׁמְעוּ אֶל מֹשֶׁה״, and it is difficult — there was no need to say anything other than ״וַיּוֹתִירוּ״ etc., and I would already know that they did not listen." "<b>Further,</b> why was it not sufficient to say ״וְלֹא שָׁמְעוּ וַיּוֹתִירוּ״, and I would know that they did not listen to the one speaking? According to our words, the interpretation of <b>וְלֹא שָׁמְעוּ</b> is fitting — that they did not listen regarding what he deduced through wisdom and forbade them of his own reasoning. And along this line we may also note precisely that the verse said in the statement preceding this one ״זֶה הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳״, whereas it did not say so in the matter before us. And the reason it bred worms and stank is that HaShem agreed with him through His hand — that he was correct in his reasoning, for the reasoning of a sage is superior to that of a prophet." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say that in truth this commandment was stated to him from the mouth of the Gevurah, except that since He had already said ״זֶה הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳״, He relied upon the fact that all the details of the laws of the manna follow from what He prefaced by saying ״זֶה הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳״. But they did not grasp his intent and thought that his instruction was the instruction of a sage, not the instruction communicated to a prophet."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:54.744867452Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}